'''Lindsey Olin Graham'''{{efn|{{IPAc-en|g|r|æ|m}} {{respell|GRAM}}}} (July 9, 1955 – July 11, 2026) was an American politician, attorney, and military officer who represented [[South Carolina]] in the [[United States Senate]] from 2003 until his death in 2026. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he represented [[South Carolina's 3rd congressional district]] in the [[U.S. House of Representatives]] from 1995 to 2003 and served in the [[South Carolina House of Representatives]] from 1993 to 1994.

Born in [[Seneca, South Carolina]], Graham graduated from the [[University of South Carolina]] and its law school. He served on active duty in the [[United States Air Force]] from 1982 to 1989 as a member of the [[United States Air Force Judge Advocate General's Corps|Judge Advocate General's Corps]], working as a defense attorney and as the Air Force's chief prosecutor in Europe. He later served in the [[South Carolina Air National Guard]] and the [[United States Air Force Reserve]], retiring as a colonel in 2015. He received the [[Bronze Star Medal]] in 2014 for his legal work relating to military operations in Iraq and Afghanistan.

Graham was first elected to Congress in [[1994 United States House of Representatives elections in South Carolina#3rd congressional district|1994]], drawing national attention as a member of the [[House Judiciary Committee]] and as a House [[impeachment manager]] during the [[Impeachment trial of Bill Clinton|impeachment trial of President Bill Clinton]]. He was elected to the Senate in [[2002 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2002]] and reelected in [[2008 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2008]], [[2014 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2014]], and [[2020 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2020]]. He won the Republican nomination for a fifth term in [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina#Initial Republican primary|June 2026]] but died before the general election a month later from an aortic dissection at the age of 71. Governor [[Henry McMaster]] appointed Graham's sister, [[Darline Graham]], to serve out the remainder of Graham's fourth term.

A [[war hawk]], Graham advocated an interventionist role for the United States and was a prominent supporter of [[Israel]], [[Ukraine]], and the [[North Atlantic Treaty Organization]]. He frequently worked with members of both parties, particularly on immigration and national-security legislation, and was closely associated with senators [[John McCain]] and [[Joe Lieberman]], with whom he formed a group known as the "Three Amigos". Graham chaired the [[Senate Judiciary Committee]] from 2019 to 2021 and the [[Senate Budget Committee]] from 2025 until his death. He also played a prominent role in the confirmation of President [[Donald Trump]]'s judicial nominees.

Graham [[Lindsey Graham 2016 presidential campaign|sought the Republican presidential nomination]] in [[2016 Republican Party presidential primaries|2016]] but withdrew before the primaries began. Initially an outspoken critic of Trump during [[Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign|Trump's 2016 campaign]], he later became one of Trump's closest congressional allies and supported him throughout both his presidencies.<ref name="Kim-2026" /><ref>{{cite news |date= July 12, 2026 |title=Lindsey Graham, US Republican senator and Trump ally, dies at 71 |url= https://www.reuters.com/world/us/us-senator-lindsey-graham-passes-away-2026-07-12/ |access-date= July 12, 2026 |work= [[Reuters]]}}</ref> 

==Early life==
Lindsey Olin Graham was born on July 9, 1955, in [[Seneca, South Carolina]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CDIR-2026-02-20/pdf/CDIR-2026-02-20.pdf |title=Official Congressional Directory, 119th Congress (2026-02-20) |author=Joint Committee on Printing |publisher=U.S. Government Publishing Office |date=2026-02-20 |access-date=2026-10-14}}</ref>. Growing up, his parents, Florence James "F.J." Graham and Millie Graham (née Walters), operated a restaurant, bar, liquor store, and pool hall in [[Central, South Carolina]]. The family lived in rooms behind the business.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/G000359|title=GRAHAM, Lindsey O.|work=[[Biographical Directory of the United States Congress]]|accessdate=July 12, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260712075334/https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/G000359|archive-date=July 12, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Summers|first=Juana|date=June 28, 2015|title=Presidential Aspiration Born From A Modest, And Tragic, Beginning|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/06/28/417837773/presidential-aspiration-born-from-a-modest-and-tragic-beginning|access-date=January 7, 2021|publisher=[[NPR]]|language=en|quote=first lived in a room behind his family's business, the Sanitary Café ...|archive-date=February 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220214090633/https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/06/28/417837773/presidential-aspiration-born-from-a-modest-and-tragic-beginning|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Hawes|first=Jennifer Berry|date=November 6, 2020|title=An unlikely ascent Love, loss, loyalty, law shape his brand of retail politics Last in polls, last in debates. How long does he go on?|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/archives/an-unlikely-ascent-love-loss-loyalty-law-shape-his-brand-of-retail-politics-last-in/article_61319af6-babe-5a4a-a561-ac7b6795b021.html|url-access=limited|access-date=January 7, 2021|website=[[Post and Courier]]|language=en|quote=His dad, Florence James Graham...He and his wife Millie ran the bar...}}</ref> His family was of [[Scotch-Irish Americans|Scotch-Irish]] descent.<ref name="Grove-1998">{{cite news |last=Grove |first=Lloyd |author-link=Lloyd Grove |date=October 7, 1998 |title=Lindsey Graham, a Twang of Moderation |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/special/clinton/stories/graham100798.htm |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |language=en-US |access-date=October 9, 2014 |archive-date=December 18, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181218062755/http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/special/clinton/stories/graham100798.htm |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.newspapers.com/clip/24250356/millie_walters_graham_1976_obituary/ |title=Millie Walters Graham, 1976, obituary, South Carolina|newspaper=[[The Greenville News]]|date=June 11, 1976|page=10|access-date=November 17, 2020|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025415/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/24250356/millie-walters-graham-1976-obituary/|url-status=live}}</ref>

After graduating from [[D. W. Daniel High School]], Graham became the first member of his family to attend college and joined the [[Reserve Officers' Training Corps]]. When he was 20, his mother died of [[Hodgkin's lymphoma]] at age 50, and his father died 15 months later of a [[heart attack]] at age 68.<ref name="Grove-1998" /> Because his sister [[Darline Graham|Darline]], then 13 years old, was left orphaned, the service allowed Graham to attend the [[University of South Carolina]] in [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]] so he could remain near home as his sister's [[legal guardian]].<ref name="Ball-2014">{{cite web|first=Molly|last=Ball|author-link=Molly Ball|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2014/06/how-lindsey-graham-stomped-the-tea-party/372521/?single_page=true|title=How Lindsey Graham Stomped the Tea Party|work=[[The Atlantic]]|location=Boston, Massachusetts|date=June 10, 2014|access-date=October 9, 2014|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025415/https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2014/06/how-lindsey-graham-stomped-the-tea-party/372521/?single_page=true|url-status=live}}</ref> During his studies, he became a member of the [[Pi Kappa Phi]] social [[fraternity]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.pikapp.org/content.aspx?id=484|title=Notable Pi Kapps|website=pikapp.org|access-date=September 28, 2020|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025420/https://pikapp.org/about/notable-pi-kappa-phis/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Graham said his parents' early deaths made him mature more quickly and Darline, who introduced her brother at his 2016 announcement of his candidacy for president, said she hoped to be with him on the campaign trail frequently to show voters his softer side. "He's kind of like a brother, a father and a mother rolled into one", she said. "I've always looked up to Lindsey."<ref>{{cite web |first= Jamie |last= Self |url= http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13855640.html |title= Lindsey Graham's sister discusses childhood, losing parents in senator's new campaign ads |work= [[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |date= May 20, 2014 |access-date= January 16, 2016 |archive-date= November 17, 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025632/https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13855640.html |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first= Alan |last= Rappeport |title= Lindsey Graham Enters White House Race With Emphasis on National Security |newspaper= [[The New York Times]] |date= June 1, 2015 |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/us/politics/lindsey-graham-presidential-campaign.html  |access-date= July 20, 2019 |archive-date= November 17, 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025611/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/06/02/us/politics/lindsey-graham-presidential-campaign.html |url-status= live }}</ref>

Graham graduated from the University of South Carolina with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in [[psychology]] in 1977. He obtained his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of South Carolina School of Law]] in 1981.<ref>{{cite news |newspaper= [[The Washington Post]]|url= http://projects.washingtonpost.com/congress/members/g000359/|title= Congress Members: Lindsey Graham| access-date= November 17, 2020| archive-date= October 29, 2014|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141029061328/http://projects.washingtonpost.com/congress/members/G000359|url-status= dead}}</ref>

==U.S. Air Force officer (1982–2015)==
[[File:Lt. Gen. Jack Rives pins Col. Lindsey Graham.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Lt. Gen. [[Jack L. Rives]] pins the [[Meritorious Service Medal (United States)|Meritorious Service Medal]] on [[Colonel (United States)|Colonel]] Lindsey Graham, April 2009]]
Upon graduating from the University of South Carolina School of Law, Graham was commissioned as an officer in the [[United States Air Force Judge Advocate General's Corps|Judge Advocate General's Corps]] (JAG Corps) in the [[United States Air Force]] in 1982 and began active duty that year. His military service began as an Air Force defense attorney. He was stationed at [[Shaw Air Force Base]] from 1982 to 1984. He was later transferred to [[Rhein-Main Air Base]] in [[Frankfurt]], [[West Germany]], where he served as the Air Force's chief prosecutor in Europe from 1984 to 1988. In 1984, as he was defending an Air Force pilot accused of using [[marijuana]], he was featured in an episode of ''[[60 Minutes]]'' that exposed the Air Force's defective drug-testing procedures.<ref name="Grove-1998"/><ref name="BaltimoreSun-2004">{{cite web |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2004/05/11/plain-talk-from-gop-senator/ |title=Plain talk from GOP senator |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=May 11, 2004 |access-date=October 12, 2014 |archive-date=October 16, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141016190753/http://articles.baltimoresun.com/2004-05-11/news/0405110360_1_lindsey-graham-graham-of-south-abuse |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Markoe-2002">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-state-graham-2-oct-2002-the-state/201548452/ |title=Graham |date=October 2, 2002 |newspaper=The State |page=A6 |last=Markoe |first=Lauren |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref> After his service in Europe, he returned to South Carolina, leaving active duty in 1989<ref name=bio/> and entering a private law practice in [[Walhalla, South Carolina|Walhalla]] with Larry Brandt.<ref name="Grove-1998"/><ref name="Markoe-2002"/> He served as assistant county attorney for [[Oconee County, South Carolina|Oconee County]] from 1988 to 1992. In 1992, he won a $5 million medical malpractice case that awarded him {{USD|200000}} in split-earnings. He was a city attorney for [[Central, South Carolina|Central]] from 1990 to 1994.<ref name="Markoe-2002"/><ref>{{cite news |date=November 9, 2020 |title=Lindsey Graham Fast Facts |url=https://www.cnn.com/2015/06/10/politics/lindsey-graham-fast-facts |publisher=CNN |location=Atlanta, GA |archive-date=April 25, 2021 |access-date=February 22, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210425165030/https://www.cnn.com/2015/06/10/politics/lindsey-graham-fast-facts |url-status=live}}</ref>

After leaving the Air Force, Graham joined the [[South Carolina Air National Guard]] in 1989, where he served until 1995, then joining the [[U.S. Air Force Reserve]].<ref name=bio>{{cite web |url=https://www.lgraham.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/biography |title=Biography |website=lgraham.senate.gov |publisher=Office of Senator Lindsey Graham |access-date=September 28, 2020 |archive-date=November 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025419/https://www.lgraham.senate.gov/public/index.cfm/biography |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 1998, the [[Capitol Hill]] daily newspaper ''[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]'' contended that Graham was describing himself on his website as an [[Operation Desert Shield]] and [[Desert Storm]] veteran. Graham responded: "I have not told anybody I'm a combatant. I'm not a war hero, and never said I was. ... If I have lied about my military record, I'm not fit to serve in Congress", further noting that he "never deployed".<ref>{{cite news|title=S.C. Rep. Gulf War Past Questioned|url=https://apnews.com/a1fcceda5e00bfa840afe5a82260c2ef|work=[[Associated Press News]]|access-date=August 10, 2014|date=February 19, 1998|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025418/https://apnews.com/a1fcceda5e00bfa840afe5a82260c2ef|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Pareene|first1=Alex|author-link=Alex Pareene|title=Lindsey Graham's war lie: Bigger than Richard Blumenthal's|url=http://www.salon.com/2010/05/20/lindsey_graham_war_liar/|access-date=June 6, 2014|work=[[Salon (website)|Salon]]|date=May 20, 2010|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025412/https://www.salon.com/2010/05/20/lindsey_graham_war_liar/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 1998, Graham was promoted to [[Lieutenant Colonel (United States)|lieutenant colonel]]. In 2004, he received his promotion to [[Colonel (United States)|colonel]] in the U.S. Air Force Reserve at a [[White House]] ceremony officiated by President [[George W. Bush]].<ref name="Whitlock-2015"/> That year, a lower court determined that Graham's service as a military judge while a sitting member of the Senate was acceptable. In 2006, the [[Court of Appeals for the Armed Forces]] set aside the lower court's ruling after concluding it was improper for Graham to serve as a military judge.<ref>''United States v. Lane'', 60 M.J. 781 (A.F. Ct. Crim. App. 2004), ''set aside by'' {{cite court |litigants=United States v. Lane |court=C.A.A.F. |date=September 20, 2006 |url=http://www.armfor.uscourts.gov/newcaaf/opinions/2006Term/05-0260.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404055846/http://www.armfor.uscourts.gov/newcaaf/opinions/2006Term/05-0260.pdf |archive-date=April 4, 2015 |url-status=live}}.</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.scotusblog.com/2006/09/ruling-senator-cannot-be-military-judge-too/ |title=Ruling: senator cannot be military judge, too |last=Denniston |first=Lyle |work=SCOTUSblog |date=2006-09-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160120193954/http://www.scotusblog.com/2006/09/ruling-senator-cannot-be-military-judge-too/ |archive-date=2016-01-20 |access-date=2026-10-18}}</ref>

In 2007, Graham served in [[Iraq]] as a reservist on active duty for a short period in April and for two weeks in August, where he worked on detainee and [[rule of law]] issues.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2007/08/27/AR2007082701919.html|title=After Tour of Duty in Iraq, Graham Backs 'Surge'|newspaper=The Washington Post|first=Robin|last=Wright|author-link=Robin Wright (author)|date=August 28, 2007|access-date=September 28, 2020|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025431/https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2007/08/27/AR2007082701919.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He also served in [[Afghanistan]] during the August 2009 Senate recess.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Thomas L. |last=Day |title=Military Notebook: Robins to hold birthday bash for Air Force |url=http://www.macon.com/197/story/834376.html |newspaper=[[The Sun News]] |date=September 6, 2009 |access-date=September 7, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090914055345/http://www.macon.com/197/story/834376.html |archive-date=September 14, 2009 |url-status=dead}}</ref> He was then assigned as a senior instructor at the [[Judge Advocate General]]'s School, though he never went.<ref name="Whitlock-2015"/>

In 2014, Graham received a [[Bronze Star Medal]] for meritorious service as a senior legal adviser to the Air Force in Iraq and Afghanistan from August 2009 to July 2014, overseeing the detention of military prisoners.<ref name="Collins-2026">{{cite news |last= Collins |first= Jeffrey |date= July 12, 2026 |title= Lindsey Graham's rise from a pool hall to global statesman |url= https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-senate-trump-88aaf34c3a2f1daa382b80b2099ccf5f |access-date= July 12, 2026 |work= [[Associated Press]] |archive-date= July 12, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260712192159/https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-senate-trump-88aaf34c3a2f1daa382b80b2099ccf5f |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Craig |last=Whitlock |title=5 Things You Should Know About Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/05/31/410358726/5-things-you-should-know-about-lindsey-graham |publisher=[[NPR]] |date=May 31, 2015 |access-date=March 25, 2019 |archive-date=July 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170704143440/http://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/05/31/410358726/5-things-you-should-know-about-lindsey-graham |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Whitlock-2015">{{cite news|first=Craig|last=Whitlock|author-link=Craig Whitlock|title=Sen. Graham moved up in Air Force Reserve ranks despite light duties|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/for-lindsey-graham-years-of-light-duty-as-a-lawmaker-in-the-air-reserve/2015/08/02/c9beb9fc-3545-11e5-adf6-7227f3b7b338_story.html|newspaper=The Washington Post|date=August 2, 2015|access-date=June 12, 2017|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025427/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/for-lindsey-graham-years-of-light-duty-as-a-lawmaker-in-the-air-reserve/2015/08/02/c9beb9fc-3545-11e5-adf6-7227f3b7b338_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2015, he retired at his last rank of [[colonel]] from the Air Force with over 33 total years of service, after reaching the statutory retirement age of 60 for his rank.<ref>{{cite news |first=Eric |last=Bradner |title=Lindsey Graham retires from Air Force ahead of expected White House run |url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/05/28/politics/lindsey-graham-retires-from-air-force/index.html |publisher=CNN|date=May 30, 2015 |access-date=May 30, 2015 |archive-date=November 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025425/https://www.cnn.com/2015/05/28/politics/lindsey-graham-retires-from-air-force/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Graham earned points toward a military pension but was unpaid as an Air Force officer while a member of Congress as he was ineligible for a military paycheck during his time in federal government service.<ref name="Whitlock-2015"/>

==South Carolina House of Representatives (1993–1994)==
In 1992, Graham was elected to the [[South Carolina House of Representatives]] from the 2nd district, in Oconee County. He defeated Democratic incumbent, [[Lowell W. Ross]], 60% to 40%,<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Cooper |first=Christopher A. |last2=Knotts |first2=Gibbs |date=2026-07-14 |title=How Lindsey Graham's keen ability to read the moment propelled him to political relevance for 3 decades |url=https://theconversation.com/how-lindsey-grahams-keen-ability-to-read-the-moment-propelled-him-to-political-relevance-for-3-decades-287471 |access-date=2026-08-14 |website=The Conversation |language=en-US}}</ref> and served one term, from 1993 to 1994.<ref>{{Cite book |url= https://scvotes.gov/files/ElectionReports/Election_Report_1992-1993.pdf |title= South Carolina Election Commission Annual Report 1992-1993 |publisher= South Carolina Election Commission |year= 1993 |location= Columbia |pages= 96| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220602134746/https://scvotes.gov/files/ElectionReports/Election_Report_1992-1993.pdf |archive-date= June 2, 2022 }}</ref> His first bill in the House sought to ban [[LGBTQ people|gays and lesbians]] from service in the [[South Carolina Army National Guard]]. It did not pass.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-state-scs-rep-lindsey-graham-enj/201550004/ |title= S.C.'s Rep. Lindsey Graham Enjoying Convention Spotlight |last= Brook |first= Nina |date= August 2, 2000 |newspaper= The State |page= 12| via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref> He proposed a bill that would impose a limit of 12 consecutive years for a state legislator. He served on the Judiciary Committee.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-greenville-news-state-house-freshman/201552016/ |title= State House Freshman to Seek Derrick's Seat |last= Berry |first= Jennifer |date= February 22, 1994 |newspaper= The Greenville News |page= 1C |via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-greenville-news-graham-22-feb-1994/201552288/ |title= Graham |last= Berry |first= Jennifer |date= February 22, 1994 |newspaper= The Greenville News |page= 2C |via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref>

==U.S. House of Representatives (1995–2003)==
===U.S. House elections===
In 1994, 20-year incumbent Democratic U.S. congressman [[Butler Derrick]] of South Carolina's northwestern-based [[South Carolina's 3rd congressional district|3rd congressional district]] decided to retire.<ref name=":0" /> Graham ran to succeed him and, with Republican U.S. senator [[Strom Thurmond]] campaigning on his behalf, won the Republican primary with 52% of the vote, defeating Bob Cantrell (33%) and Ed Allgood (15%).<ref name="SCEC-1995">{{Cite book |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1994-1995.pdf |title=Election Report 1994-1995 |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |year=1995 |location=Columbia |pages= |access-date=April 28, 2024 |archive-date=August 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240802060253/https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1994-1995.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>{{Rp|page=18}} In the general election, Graham defeated Democratic State Senator James Bryan Jr., 60% to 40%.<ref name="SCEC-1995" />{{Rp|page=29}} As a part of that year's [[Republican Revolution]], Graham campaigned in support of [[Newt Gingrich]]'s [[Contract with America]] legislative agenda and became the first Republican to represent the district since 1877.<ref name="Draper-2010">{{cite news|first=Robert|last=Draper|author-link=Robert Draper|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/07/04/magazine/04graham-t.html |title=Lindsey Graham, This Year's Maverick|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=July 1, 2010|access-date=October 9, 2014|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025412/https://www.nytimes.com/2010/07/04/magazine/04graham-t.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Welch-2002">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/usa-today-impeachment-manager-wins-7-no/201550643/ |title=Impeachment Manager Wins |last=Welch |first=William M. |date=November 7, 2002 |newspaper=[[USA Today]] |page=2 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref>

In 1996, he was challenged by Debbie Dorn, the niece of Butler Derrick and daughter of Derrick's predecessor, 13-term Democratic congressman [[William Jennings Bryan Dorn]]. Graham was reelected, defeating Dorn 60% to 40%.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1995-1996.pdf |title=South Carolina Election Report 1995-1996 |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |year=1997 |location=Columbia |page=40 |access-date=April 28, 2024 |archive-date=August 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240802060246/https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1995-1996.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1998, he was reelected to a third term unopposed.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1997-1998.pdf |title=South Carolina Election Report 1997 & 1998 |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |year=1999 |location=Columbia |page=46 |access-date=April 28, 2024 |archive-date=August 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240802060239/https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1997-1998.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2000, he was reelected to a fourth term, defeating Democratic nominee George Brightharp, 68% to 30%.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_2000.pdf |title=South Carolina Election Report 2000 |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |year=2001 |location=Columbia |page=37 |access-date=April 28, 2024 |archive-date=August 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240802060232/https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_2000.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== U.S. House tenure ===
[[File:December 18 1998 house session c span program.136261.MP4D20.webm|thumb|start=02:31:36|thumbtime=02:32:21|Graham speaking during Clinton's impeachment on December 18, 1998, and also discussing the grounds for impeachment of [[Richard Nixon]] and other presidents]]
In 1994, Graham, along with other Republicans elected that year, promised to serve no more than 12 years in office.<ref name="Markoe-2002"/> In 1995, he was one of five Republicans to vote against settling a budget standoff that caused a [[Government shutdowns in the United States|government shutdown]].<ref name="Welch-2002"/> In 1997, he took part in [[Newt Gingrich#Leadership challenge|a leadership challenge]] against [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|House Speaker]] Newt Gingrich.<ref name="Grove-1998"/><ref name="Markoe-2002"/>

In November 1997, Graham was one of 18 House Republicans to co-sponsor [[1997 impeachment resolution by Bob Barr|a resolution]] by [[Bob Barr]] that sought to launch an [[Impeachment inquiry in the United States|impeachment inquiry]] into President [[Bill Clinton]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pace |first1=David |title=17 in House seek probe to impeach president |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-record-17-in-house-seek-probe-to-imp/193124088/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Record (North Jersey)|The Record]] | agency=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=March 4, 2021 |language=en |date=November 6, 1997}}</ref><ref name="Hutcheson-1997"/> The resolution did not specify any charges or allegations.<ref name="Hutcheson-1997">{{cite news |last1=Hutcheson |first1=Ron |title=Some House Republicans can't wait for elections |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/asheville-citizen-times-some-house-repub/193124106/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[Asheville Citizen-Times]] |agency=Knight-Rider Newspapers |language=en |date=November 17, 1997}}</ref> This was an early effort to [[Federal impeachment in the United States|impeach]] Clinton, predating the eruption of the [[Clinton–Lewinsky scandal]]. The eruption of that scandal ultimately led to a more serious effort to impeach Clinton in 1998.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Barkham |first1=Patrick |title=Clinton impeachment timeline |url= https://www.theguardian.com/world/1998/nov/18/clinton.usa |website=The Guardian |access-date= March 6, 2021 |language=en |date=November 18, 1998 |archive-date=February 28, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210228133624/http://www.theguardian.com/world/1998/nov/18/clinton.usa |url-status=live }}</ref> On October 8, 1998, Graham voted in favor of legislation to open [[Impeachment inquiry against Bill Clinton|an impeachment inquiry]].<ref name="Ball-2014"/><ref>{{cite web |title=Roll Call 498 Roll Call 498, Bill Number: H. Res. 581, 105th Congress, 2nd Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998498 |website= clerk.house.gov |publisher=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 1, 2021 |language=en |date=October 8, 1998 |archive-date=April 13, 2021 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210413150937/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998498 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was a member of the [[United States House Committee on the Judiciary|Judiciary Committee]], which conducted the inquiry.<ref>{{cite news|title= The Education Of Senator Lindsey Graham|url=http://www.businessweek.com/stories/2005-02-27/the-education-of-senator-lindsey-graham |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20121022083915/http://www.businessweek.com/stories/2005-02-27/the-education-of-senator-lindsey-graham|url-status= dead| archive-date= October 22, 2012| access-date= June 6, 2014| work= [[Bloomberg Businessweek]] |date= February 27, 2005}}</ref>

In both the Judiciary Committee vote on forwarding proposed [[articles of impeachment]], and the full House vote on the proposed articles of impeachment, Graham voted for three of the four proposed articles of impeachment. He voted against the second count of perjury in the [[Paula Jones]] case.<ref name="RC546">{{cite web |title=Roll Call 546 Roll Call 546, Bill Number: H. Res. 611, 105th Congress, 2nd Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998546 |website=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 6, 2021 |language=en |date=December 19, 1998 |archive-date=November 25, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125214436/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998546 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="RC545">{{cite web |title=Roll Call 545 Roll Call 545, Bill Number: H. Res. 611, 105th Congress, 2nd Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998545 |website=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 6, 2021 |language=en |date=December 19, 1998 |archive-date=November 25, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125214517/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998545 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="RC544">{{cite web |title=Roll Call 544 Roll Call 544, Bill Number: H. Res. 611, 105th Congress, 2nd Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998544 |website=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 6, 2021 |language=en |date=December 19, 1998 |archive-date=January 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126161740/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998544 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="RC543">{{cite web |title=Roll Call 543, Bill Number: H. Res. 611, 105th Congress, 2nd Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998543 |website=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 6, 2021 |language=en |date=December 19, 1998 |archive-date=March 3, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210303091343/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1998543 |url-status=live }}</ref> This made him the only Republican on the Judiciary Committee to vote against any of the proposed articles of impeachment.<ref name="Ball-2014"/> During the inquiry, Graham asked, "Is this [[Watergate]] or Peyton Place?"<ref name="Ball-2014"/><ref name="Grove-1998"/> The House [[Impeachment of Bill Clinton|passed two of the impeachment articles]].<ref name="RC545"/><ref name="RC543"/> Graham served as a [[House impeachment manager]] in [[Impeachment trial of Bill Clinton|the impeachment trial]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Prosecution Who's Who |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/special/clinton/prosecution.htm |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=February 28, 2021 |date=January 14, 1999 |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028113121/https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/politics/special/clinton/prosecution.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 1999 and 2001, Graham sponsored the Unborn Victims of Violence Act in the House. It passed by margins of 254–172 and 252–172 in 1999 and 2001, respectively. The bill sought to make it a federal crime to harm a fetus during an assault of its mother, except by medical professionals with the mother's consent.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-greenville-news-house-oks-fetus-righ/201554366/ |title= House OKs Fetus Rights Bill |date= October 1, 1999 |newspaper= The Greenville News |page= 1A |via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-index-journal-house-backs-fetus-har/201552683/ |title= House Backs 'Fetus Harm' Bill |date= April 27, 2001 |newspaper= The Index-Journal |page= 1 |via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref> In both cases, the Senate did not take up the bill for a vote. In 2004, the similar [[Unborn Victims of Violence Act]] was signed into law.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.newspapers.com/article/florence-morning-news-measure-27-feb-20/201553947/ |title= Measure |date= February 27, 2004 |newspaper= Florence Morning News |page= 6A |via= [[Newspapers.com]] |access-date= July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref>

After the [[September 11 attacks]], Graham voted for the [[Patriot Act]] and the [[Authorization for Use of Military Force of 2001]].<ref>{{Cite news |date= October 24, 2001 |title= Roll Call 398 Roll Call 398, Bill Number: H. R. 3162, 107th Congress, 1st Session |url= https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2001398 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260501090125/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2001398 |archive-date= May 1, 2026 |access-date= July 13, 2026 |work= Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |language= en |url-status= live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date= September 14, 2001 |title= Roll Call 342 Roll Call 342, Bill Number: H. J. Res. 64, 107th Congress, 1st Session |url= https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2001342 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260606174751/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2001342 |archive-date= June 6, 2026 |access-date= July 13, 2026 |work= Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |language= en |url-status= live }}</ref> In 2002, Graham voted for the [[Iraq resolution]].<ref>{{Cite news |date= October 10, 2002 |title= Roll Call 455 Roll Call 455, Bill Number: H. J. Res. 114, 107th Congress, 2nd Session |url= https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2002455 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260317114558/https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2002455 |archive-date= March 17, 2026 |access-date= July 13, 2026 |work= Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |language= en |url-status= live }}</ref>

=== U.S. House committee assignments ===
During his service in the House, Graham had served on the following committees:
* [[United States House Committee on International Relations|Committee on International Relations]] (1995–1998)<ref name="HouseCommittees"/>
* [[Committee on Education and the Workforce]] (1995–2002)<ref name="HouseCommittees">{{cite web |title=Standing Committees of the House |url=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CDIR-1997-06-04/html/CDIR-1997-06-04-HOUSECOMMITTEES.htm |access-date=July 12, 2026 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206044347/https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CDIR-1997-06-04/html/CDIR-1997-06-04-HOUSECOMMITTEES.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[United States House Committee on the Judiciary|Committee on the Judiciary]] (1997–2002)<ref>{{cite web |title=Report of the Activities of the Committee of the Judiciary |url=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-105hrpt845/html/CRPT-105hrpt845.htm |access-date=July 12, 2026 |archive-date=February 22, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230222203312/https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-105hrpt845/html/CRPT-105hrpt845.htm |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[United States House Committee on Armed Services|Committee on National Security]] (1999–2002)<ref>{{cite web |title=Report of the Activities of the Committee on National Security |url=http://govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-105hrpt841/html/CRPT-105hrpt841.htm |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

==U.S. Senate (2003–2026)==
[[File:Lindsey Graham, US Senate Portrait.jpg|thumb|Official portrait, 2006]]
===Senate elections===
====2002====
{{Main|2002 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
In 2001, [[Bob Dole]] signed on as the chairman of the national steering committee for Graham's campaign for the U.S. Senate.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-times-and-democrat-bob-dole-to-join/201551640/ |title=Bob Dole to Join Lindsey Graham's Senate Campaign |date=March 7, 2001 |newspaper=The Times and Democrat |page=4 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=July 13, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref> In 2002, longtime U.S. senator [[Strom Thurmond]] decided to retire.<ref name=":0" /> Graham ran to succeed him and won the Republican primary unopposed. In the general election, he defeated Democratic nominee [[Alex Sanders (politician)|Alex Sanders]], former president of the [[College of Charleston]] and former chief judge of the [[South Carolina Court of Appeals]], 54.40% to 44.19%.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Trandahl |first=Jeff |url=https://history.house.gov/Institution/Election-Statistics/ |title=Statistics of the Congressional Election of November 5, 2002 |publisher=United States House of Representatives |year=2003 |location=Washington, D.C. |pages=42 |archive-date=December 17, 2020 |access-date=September 11, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217170227/https://history.house.gov/Institution/Election-Statistics/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Before the election, Graham promised to serve no more than two six-year terms in office.<ref name="Markoe-2002"/> He became South Carolina's first new U.S. senator since [[Fritz Hollings]] in 1966.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/8562/13358/en/summary.html|title=SC – Election Results|work=enr-scvotes.org|access-date=November 7, 2008|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025436/https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/8562/15723/en/summary.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2008====
{{Main|2008 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
Graham ran for a second term in 2008, and was challenged in the Republican primary by National Executive Committeeman of the [[South Carolina Republican Party]], Buddy Witherspoon. Graham won renomination, 66.82% to 33.18%, winning all but one of South Carolina's 46 counties.<ref>{{Cite web |date= June 27, 2008 |title= Statewide Results – 2008 Republican and Democratic Primary |url= https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/4186/7382/en/summary.html |access-date= September 10, 2022 |website= South Carolina State Election Commission |archive-date= November 17, 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025606/https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/4186/7382/en/summary.html |url-status= live }}</ref>

In the general election, Graham defeated Democratic nominee Bob Conley, a pilot and engineer, 57.53% to 42.25%. He outspent Conley, $6.6 million to $15,000.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.opensecrets.org/races/summary.php?id=SCS2&cycle=2008 |title=South Carolina Senate 2008 Race |website= OpenSecrets.org |publisher= [[OpenSecrets]] |access-date= October 9, 2014 |archive-date= November 17, 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025421/http://www.opensecrets.org/races//summary?id=SCS2&cycle=2008 |url-status= live }}</ref>

====2014====
{{Main|2014 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
Graham ran for reelection in 2014. Of the Republican senators up for reelection that year, he was considered one of the most vulnerable to a primary challenge, largely due to his low approval ratings and reputation for working with and compromising with Democrats.<ref name="Kludt-2014">{{cite web |first=Tom |last=Kludt |url=http://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/lindsey-graham-faces-big-weak-field-challengers-n74321 |title=Lindsey Graham Faces Big – But Weak – Field of Challengers |publisher=[[NBC News]] |date=April 8, 2014 |access-date=April 24, 2014 |archive-date=April 24, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140424070735/http://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/lindsey-graham-faces-big-weak-field-challengers-n74321 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Tom |last=Kludt |url=http://talkingpointsmemo.com/livewire/poll-lindsey-graham-s-approval-rating-tumbles-in-s-c |title=Poll: Lindsey Graham's Approval Rating Tumbles In S.C |work=[[Talking Points Memo]] |date=October 30, 2013 |access-date=February 14, 2014 |archive-date=September 11, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170911004308/http://talkingpointsmemo.com/livewire/poll-lindsey-graham-s-approval-rating-tumbles-in-s-c |url-status=live }}</ref> He expected a primary challenge from conservative activists, including the [[Tea Party movement]], and [[Chris Chocola]], President of the [[Club for Growth]], said his organization would support a primary challenge if an acceptable standard-bearer emerged.<ref>{{cite web |last=Rosen |first=James |author-link=James Rosen (author) |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/article14399300.html |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160210081911/https://www.thestate.com/news/article14399300.html |work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |title=Graham: 'I expect' a primary challenge |date=February 19, 2012 |access-date=September 28, 2020 |archive-date=February 10, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Sullivan |first=Sean |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2012/09/20/lindsey-graham-2014-target/ |title=Lindsey Graham, 2014 target? |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=September 20, 2012 |access-date=April 24, 2014 |archive-date=May 14, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150514052517/http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2012/09/20/lindsey-graham-2014-target/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

But no serious challenger to Graham emerged, and he was widely viewed as likely to win,<ref name="Killian-2014">{{cite web|first=Linda|last=Killian|url=https://blogs.wsj.com/washwire/2014/06/10/lindsey-graham-vs-the-tea-party/|title=Lindsey Graham vs. the Tea Party|work=[[The Wall Street Journal]]|location=New York City|date=June 10, 2014|access-date=October 11, 2014|archive-date=June 14, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140614205358/https://blogs.wsj.com/washwire/2014/06/10/lindsey-graham-vs-the-tea-party/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Murphy-2014">{{cite news|first=Patricia|last=Murphy|url=http://www.thedailybeast.com/articles/2014/06/10/lindsey-graham-s-tea-party-teflon.html|title=Lindsey Graham's Tea Party Teflon|work=[[The Daily Beast]]|date=June 10, 2014|access-date=October 11, 2014|archive-date=October 17, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141017004053/http://www.thedailybeast.com/articles/2014/06/10/lindsey-graham-s-tea-party-teflon.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Kludt-2014"/> which has been ascribed to his "deft maneuvering" and "aggressive" response to the challenge. He befriended potential opponents from the state's congressional delegation and helped them with fundraising and securing their preferred committee assignments. He assembled a "daunting multimillion-dollar political operation" dubbed the "Graham machine" that built six regional offices across the state and enlisted the support of thousands of paid staffers and volunteers, including over 5,000 [[precinct captain]]s; built a "staggering" campaign war chest and "blanketed" the state with ads; focused on constituent services and local issues; and refused to "pander" to Tea Party supporters, instead confronting them head-on, arguing that the Republican Party needed to be more inclusive.<ref name="Raju-2014">{{cite web|first=Manu|last=Raju|author-link=Manu Raju|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2014/04/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-tea-party-105928.html|title=How Lindsey Graham outmaneuvered the tea party|work=[[Politico]]|date=April 23, 2014|access-date=April 24, 2014|archive-date=April 24, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140424015145/http://www.politico.com/story/2014/04/lindsey-graham-south-carolina-tea-party-105928.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Ball-2014"/>

In the run-up to the Republican primary, Graham's approval rating improved. A February 2013 Winthrop poll found that he had a 59% positive rating among likely Republican voters.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Clement|first1=Scott|title=Poll: Lindsey Graham trouncing crowded primary field|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/02/26/poll-lindsey-graham-trouncing-crowded-primary-field/|access-date=August 13, 2014|newspaper=The Washington Post|date=February 26, 2014|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025433/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/post-politics/wp/2014/02/26/poll-lindsey-graham-trouncing-crowded-primary-field/?arc404=true|url-status=live}}</ref>

In June 2014, Graham won the Republican primary with 56.42% of the vote in a crowded field. His closest challenger, State Senator [[Lee Bright]], received 15.4%. His other primary opponents included [[Richard Cash (politician)|Richard Cash]] and [[Nancy Mace]].<ref>{{cite news |title=South Carolina Primary Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/2014/results/primaries/south-carolina |work=The New York Times |access-date=November 17, 2019 |archive-date=November 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025426/https://www.nytimes.com/elections/2014/results/primaries/south-carolina |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the general election, Graham defeated the Democratic nominee, State Senator [[Brad Hutto]], 54% to 39%. Independent [[Thomas Ravenel]] (a former Republican State Treasurer), and Libertarian Victor Kocher received 3.8% and 2.7% of the vote, respectively.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/53424/149231/en/summary.html#|title=SC – Election Results|work=enr-scvotes.org}}</ref>

====2020====
{{Main|2020 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}

Graham ran for reelection in 2020, winning renomination in the Republican primary with 67.69% of the vote against three other candidates.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/103402/Web02-state.250556/#/cid/26001|title=U.S. SENATE – REP|access-date=June 9, 2020|publisher=[[Secretary of State of South Carolina]]|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025615/https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/103402/Web02-state.250556/#/cid/26001|url-status=live}}</ref>

Democrat [[Jaime Harrison]] challenged Graham in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.com/lindsey-graham-tied-democrat-south-carolina-senate-challenger-jaime-harrison-poll-1477336|title=Lindsey Graham's Democratic Challenger Nearly Tied in Heavily-Republican South Carolina, Poll Shows|first=Benjamin|last=Fearnow|date=December 15, 2019|work=[[Newsweek]]|access-date=September 28, 2020|archive-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025422/https://www.newsweek.com/lindsey-graham-tied-democrat-south-carolina-senate-challenger-jaime-harrison-poll-1477336|url-status=live}}</ref> The race was considered potentially competitive, with several polls in its final months showing a close race and Harrison posting record fundraising numbers, but Graham won by more than 10 points, 54.4% to 44.2%.<ref>[https://www.politico.com/news/2020/11/03/south-carolina-senate-election-results-2020-433581 Graham fends off Harrison in South Carolina Senate race] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117025426/https://www.politico.com/news/2020/11/03/south-carolina-senate-election-results-2020-433581 |date=November 17, 2020 }}, ''[[Politico]]'', November 3, 2020.</ref>

==== 2026 ====
{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}

Graham ran for reelection in 2026. On January 14, 2025, he announced lead officials for his campaign, including the former chairman of the South Carolina Republican Party and Governor [[Henry McMaster]]'s campaign manager.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Connaughton |first=Kevin |date=January 14, 2025 |title=Lindsey Graham announces campaign team, funding for 2026 re-election bid |url=https://www.wistv.com/2025/01/14/lindsey-graham-announces-campaign-team-funding-2026-re-election-bid/ |access-date=January 27, 2025 |website=wistv.com |language=en |archive-date=January 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250127002514/https://www.wistv.com/2025/01/14/lindsey-graham-announces-campaign-team-funding-2026-re-election-bid/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He reported $15.6 million in campaign fundraising. Trump endorsed Graham on March 26, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nitzberg |first=Alex |date=March 27, 2025 |title=Trump endorses Sen. Lindsey Graham for re-election: 'HE WILL NOT LET YOU DOWN' |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/trump-endorses-sen-lindsey-graham-re-election-he-not-let-you-down |access-date=April 5, 2025 |publisher=Fox News |language=en-US |archive-date=April 5, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250405021658/https://www.foxnews.com/politics/trump-endorses-sen-lindsey-graham-re-election-he-not-let-you-down |url-status=live }}</ref>

At the time of his death, Graham was [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina|running for reelection]] to a fifth Senate term as the Republican nominee. On June 9, 2026, he had won the Republican primary with 56.8% of the vote against five other candidates.<ref name="NYT-2026">{{Cite news |date=June 9, 2026 |title=South Carolina U.S. Senate Primary 2026: Live Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/us/elections/results-south-carolina-us-senate-primary.html |access-date=June 11, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Graham died on July 11, and, on July 13, South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster appointed Graham's sister, [[Darline Graham]], to serve the remainder of his fourth term.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rainey |first=Zach |date=July 13, 2026 |title=Darline Graham Nordone named to finish out Sen. Lindsey Graham's term |url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/lindsey-graham-senate-replacement-darline-nordone/71920171 |access-date=July 13, 2026 |website=WYFF |language=en}}</ref> Darline Graham went on to win the [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina#Special Republican primary|special primary runoff]], and became the Republican nominee.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2026-08-26 |title=Lindsey Graham's sister wins Republican primary to contest Senate seat in November |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8r6dxxy27eo |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

