==Personal life==
Referred to in a ''[[Washington Post]]'' headline as a "[[confirmed bachelor]]",<ref>{{Cite news|last= Andrews-Dyer |first= Helena |title= Confirmed bachelor and 2016 hopeful Lindsey Graham promises a 'rotating first lady' |newspaper= [[The Washington Post]] |language= en-US |date= June 9, 2015 |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/reliable-source/wp/2015/06/09/confirmed-bachelor-and-2016-hopeful-lindsey-graham-promises-a-rotating-first-lady/ |access-date= July 12, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=#LadyGraham went viral – and not just because of Lindsey Graham's politics|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2020/06/10/ladygraham-went-viral-not-just-because-lindsey-grahams-politics/|newspaper=The Washington Post|date=June 10, 2020|last=Balcerski|first=Thomas|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Graham denied being gay.<ref>{{Cite web |title=If Lindsey Graham was gay, Pride does not claim him|work=The Advocate|first=John|last=Casey|url=https://www.advocate.com/opinion/lindsey-graham-gay-rumors-death|date=July 14, 2026 |access-date=July 18, 2026  |language=en}}</ref> While never publicly romantically linked to a woman,<ref>{{Cite web|title= The Complicated Legacy of Lindsey Graham |url=https://slate.com/life/2026/07/lindsey-graham-death-trump-gay-married.html|website=[[Slate (magazine)]]|date=July 14, 2026|last=Cauterucci|first=Christina|access-date=July 16, 2026|url-access=subscription}}</ref> he wrote in his memoir that he had a girlfriend in law school named Debbie, and two more during his time in the Air Force in Germany: a JAG officer named Carol, who later served on [[Colin Powell]]'s staff, and a flight attendant named Sylvia, to whom he considered proposing.<ref>{{Cite web|title= Remembering Lindsey Graham: His Life, Family, and Reasons for Never Marrying|url= https://www.aol.com/articles/remembering-lindsey-graham-life-family-142009000.html|website=[[AOL]]|date=July 12, 2026|last=McBride|first=Jessica|access-date=July 16, 2026|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260716164901/https://www.aol.com/articles/remembering-lindsey-graham-life-family-142009000.html|archive-date=July 16, 2026}}</ref> He wrote, "I never found time to meet the right girl, or the right girl was smart enough not to have time for me."<ref>{{cite magazine |last= McAfee |first= Tierney |date= June 17, 2015 |title= Presidential Candidate Lindsey Graham Explains Why He Never Married in New Memoir |url= https://people.com/books/lindsey-graham-memoir-my-story-explains-why-he-never-married/ |magazine= [[People (magazine)|People]] |access-date= February 14, 2026}}</ref> On the ''[[I've Had It (podcast)|I've Had It]]'' podcast shortly after Graham's death, [[Hunter Biden]] noted the friendship between his father [[Joe Biden]] and Graham<ref>{{cite web| last= Sommerlad| first= Joe| date= July 13, 2026 |title= Hunter Biden says he prefers to remember Lindsey Graham as he was 'before Trump' |url= https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/lindsey-graham-hunter-biden-trump-b3013768.html |magazine= [[The Independent]] |access-date=July 21, 2026}}</ref> and said that "of course" Graham was gay.<ref>{{Cite tweet |user= CalltoActivism |number= 2079526982245642448| title= Wow. Hunter Biden, who knew Lindsey Graham for decades as a close family friend, not only outs him as gay - but says Washington is ran by (sic) a "mafia" of closeted gay Republicans.}}</ref> In October 2026, progressive and left-wing populist media organization [[The Young Turks]] released a documentary interviewing three [[Male prostitution|male former sex workers]] who alleged that Graham was their client, including one who said he was 17 at the time of the alleged encounter.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hazard |first1=Charlotte |title=The Young Turks releases video alleging Lindsey Graham had sexual relationships with men |url=https://katu.com/news/nation-world/the-young-turks-releases-video-alleging-lindsey-graham-had-sexual-relationships-with-men-darline-graham-south-carolina |access-date=22 October 2026 |work=[[KATU]] |agency=[[The National News Desk]] |date=October 22, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wiggins |first1=Christopher |title=Lindsey Graham opposed LGBTQ+ rights. Three men say he paid them for sex |url=https://www.advocate.com/politics/national/lindsey-graham-male-escorts-documentary |access-date=22 October 2026 |work=Advocate |date=22 October 2026}}</ref>

Graham had residences in Washington, D.C., and [[Seneca, South Carolina]]. A [[Southern Baptist]], he was a member of Seneca's Corinth Baptist Church.<ref name=bio/>

== Death ==
[[File:Desk of Senator Lindsey Graham.jpg|thumb|right|Graham's desk on the Senate floor following his death]]
Graham died after a "brief and sudden illness" on the evening of July 11, 2026,<ref name="Diamond-2026">{{Cite news |last=Diamond |first=Dan |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Lindsey Graham, longtime senator from South Carolina, dies at 71 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/12/lindsey-graham-longtime-south-carolina-senator-dies-71/ |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kim |first1=Seung Min |last2=Jalonick |first2=Mary Clare |last3=Kinnard |first3=Meg |date=July 12, 2026 |title=US Senator Lindsey Graham has died after a brief and unexpected illness, his office says |url=https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-dies-south-carolina-bfa556e170f2df22ce9ffc7165da3dfa |website=Associated Press |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> a day after returning from an official visit to [[Kyiv]], Ukraine.<ref name="Diamond-2026"/> [[Emergency medical services]] responded to a call at a home owned by Graham at around 8:30 p.m. [[Eastern Time Zone|EDT]] in the [[Capitol Hill]] section of Washington, D.C. for a patient with chest pain. According to radio transmissions, personnel reported 25 minutes later that a male was in [[cardiac arrest]] and that [[CPR]] was under way. On July 12, preliminary findings from the D.C. [[medical examiner]] stated that Graham died from an [[aortic dissection]] caused by [[arteriosclerotic cardiovascular disease]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kim |first1=Seung Min |last2=Jalonick |first2=Mary Clare |last3=Kinnard |first3=Meg |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham likely died after aorta rupture, medical examiner says |url=https://apnews.com/article/lindsey-graham-dies-south-carolina-bfa556e170f2df22ce9ffc7165da3dfa |work=Associated Press |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gillum |first1=Jack |last2=Gurman |first2=Sadie |last3=Hughes |first3=Siobhan |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Graham Died of Aortic Tear, According to Preliminary Findings |url=https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/what-dispatch-audio-tells-us-about-the-emergency-response-at-lindsey-grahams-home-653408a7 |work=The Wall Street Journal |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

=== Reactions ===

==== Domestic ====
Upon the news of Graham's death, President [[Donald Trump]] called him a "true American Patriot" and said he was "like a member of the family".<ref>{{Cite news |last1= Main |first1= Alison |last2= Tsui |first2= Karina |last3= Fox |first3= Lauren |last4= Fox |first4= Kara |date= July 12, 2026 |title= Lindsey Graham, longtime GOP senator and Trump ally, dies at 71 after sudden illness |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/12/politics/sen-lindsey-graham-dies-at-71-hnk |website= CNN |language= en-US |access-date= July 12, 2026}}</ref> Trump later said he spoke with Graham the night before his death about the [[SAVE America Act]], describing him as a close ally and friend who could work across party lines.<ref>{{cite news |last=Brand |first=Megan |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Trump says he spoke with Sen. Lindsey Graham hours before his death |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/trump-phone-call-lindsey-graham-death-save-act-rcna552732 |website= NBC News |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Former presidents [[Joe Biden]] and [[George W. Bush]] also honored Graham's public service. Biden recalled their years working together in the Senate, saying they often disagreed but shared a commitment to public service and respect for the institution.<ref name="CNN-2026">{{cite web |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Remembering Lindsey Graham: Tributes pour in for the late senator |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2026/07/12/politics/live-news/lindsey-graham-tributes-death-updates |website=CNN |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Bush praised Graham as a knowledgeable senator who understood America's role in the world and was dedicated to his country.<ref name="Hubbard-2026">{{cite web |last1=Hubbard |first1=Kaia |last2=Watson |first2=Kathryn |date=July 12, 2026 |title=U.S. and world leaders pay tribute to Lindsey Graham following sudden death |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/lindsey-graham-us-world-leaders-pay-tribute-following-sudden-death-trump-netanyahu/ |website=CBS News |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

Graham's colleagues in Congress also paid tribute. [[U.S. Senate Majority Leader]] [[John Thune]] called him a strong advocate for the United States, while fellow South Carolina senator [[Tim Scott]] said America had lost a statesman and he had lost a friend.<ref>{{Cite news |last= Fields |first= Ashleigh |date= July 12, 2026 |title= Sen. Lindsey Graham dies of 'brief and sudden illness' |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/5964424-lindsey-graham-sudden-death/ |website=The Hill |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> McMaster called Graham "the fiercest of fighters for South Carolina and America".<ref name="Diamond-2026" /> Vice President [[JD Vance]] recalled disagreements with Graham over Ukraine funding but praised him as someone who fought for his beliefs and supported others when it mattered.<ref name="Schneid-2026">{{cite magazine |last=Schneid |first=Rebecca |date=July 12, 2026 |title=The World Remembers Senator Lindsey Graham |url=https://time.com/article/2026/07/12/the-world-pays-tribute-to-sen-lindsey-graham/ |magazine=Time |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> House Speaker [[Mike Johnson]] praised Graham's lifelong service and commitment to "American strength and exceptionalism".<ref name="Hutchinson-2026">{{cite web |last=Hutchinson |first=Bill |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham's death elicits bipartisan outpouring of condolences |url=https://abcnews.com/Politics/sen-lindsey-grahams-death-prompts-bipartisan-outpouring-condolences/story?id=134693549 |website=ABC News |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

Republican members of South Carolina's congressional delegation also mourned Graham's death. [[Nancy Mace]] called him "funny", "smart", and "a force to be reckoned with", while [[Joe Wilson]] called him an "American patriot". [[Ralph Norman]] remembered him as a "son of South Carolina" who deeply loved the state, and [[Russell Fry]] praised him as an "extraordinary public servant" and consequential leader whose legacy would be measured by the many people he helped.<ref name="Hubbard-2026" />

Democratic senators remembered Graham's bipartisan approach and willingness to work across party lines. Senators [[Richard Blumenthal]], [[Mark Warner]], and [[Dick Durbin]] highlighted his work on Ukraine, Russia sanctions, and Senate negotiations.<ref name="Hubbard-2026" /> [[Amy Klobuchar]] praised his commitment to public service, willingness to tackle difficult issues, and efforts to assist [[Afghan refugees]]. [[Elizabeth Warren]], [[Cory Booker]], and [[Adam Schiff]] also recalled his ability to negotiate despite disagreements, citing his bipartisan work on criminal justice reform, national security, and Ukraine.<ref name="CNN-2026" /> [[Chris Coons]], who spent Graham's 71st birthday with him during the [[2026 NATO summit]] in Turkey, called him "no better friend, no tougher adversary" and urged Congress to honor his memory through legislation.<ref name="CNN-2026" /> [[James Clyburn]] highlighted their relationship built on mutual respect despite political differences.<ref name="Schneid-2026" /> Former vice president [[Kamala Harris]] remembered Graham as a witty, energetic colleague who cared deeply about the Senate and South Carolina.<ref name="Hutchinson-2026" />

==== International ====
[[President of the European Commission]] [[Ursula von der Leyen]] offered her condolences to Graham's family and called him "a determined and fearless leader".<ref>{{Cite tweet |author=Ursula von der Leyen |user=vonderleyen |number=2076220916955021693 |title=My condolences to Senator Lindsey Graham's family and loved ones.}}</ref> Ukrainian president [[Volodymyr Zelenskyy]] released a statement offering his condolences, saying: "America and the world have lost a resolute leader."<ref>{{Cite news |title=Zelensky Expresses Condolences Over Death of US Senator Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.kyivpost.com/post/80115 |website=Kyiv Post |language=en |date=July 12, 2026 |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> [[Mark Rutte]], [[secretary general of NATO]], said Graham was "a powerful advocate for America"; German chancellor [[Friedrich Merz]] said he was "a true friend and partner of Germany in the transatlantic alliance".<ref>{{Cite web |last= Moulson |first= Geir |date= July 12, 2026 |title= Lindsey Graham draws tributes for his support of Ukraine, trans-Atlantic ties and Israel |url= https://www.dallasnews.com/news/article/lindsey-graham-draws-tributes-for-his-support-of-22342056.php |website= The Dallas Morning News |language= en-US |access-date= July 12, 2026}}</ref> Finnish president [[Alexander Stubb]] mourned Graham as a "personal friend", praising his commitment to NATO, transatlantic cooperation, and support for Ukraine.<ref name="Schneid-2026" /> Estonian foreign minister [[Margus Tsahkna]], Latvian foreign minister [[Baiba Braže]], and Lithuanian foreign minister [[Kęstutis Budrys]] jointly paid tribute to Graham, remembering him as a steadfast friend and a strong supporter of NATO, transatlantic relations, and Ukraine.<ref name="AP-2026">{{cite web |title=World leaders pay tribute to Sen. Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/07/12/world-leaders-pay-tribute-sen-lindsey-graham/ |website=Fox Carolina |language=en-US |date=July 12, 2026 |access-date=July 12, 2026 |publisher=Associated Press}}</ref> Canadian prime minister [[Mark Carney]] said Graham "stood resolutely in defence of democracy and freedom".<ref>{{cite news |title=Carney says U.S. Sen. Lindsey Graham was steadfast defender of democracy and freedom |url= https://www.ctvnews.ca/politics/article/carney-says-us-sen-lindsey-graham-was-steadfast-defender-of-democracy-and-freedom/ |website=CTV News |language=en-CA |date=July 12, 2026 |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

[[Prime Minister of Israel]] [[Benjamin Netanyahu]] said: "Israel has lost one of its greatest friends. America has lost a great patriot. I have lost a beloved friend." He offered his condolences to Graham's family and the American people.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Berman |first=Lazar |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Netanyahu on Graham: 'Israel has lost one of its greatest friends, America has lost a great patriot' |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/netanyahu-on-graham-america-has-lost-a-great-patriot-i-have-lost-a-beloved-friend/ |website=The Times of Israel |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Netanyahu later recalled one of his final conversations with Graham, saying that Graham had strongly opposed any reduction of [[U.S. military aid to Israel]] and viewed support for Israel's security as connected to U.S. interests.<ref>{{cite web |last=Penley |first=Taylor |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Netanyahu details 'final' conversation with Lindsey Graham, says senator challenged his push to end Israel aid |url=https://www.foxnews.com/media/netanyahu-details-final-conversation-lindsey-graham-senator-challenged-push-end-israel-aid |website=Fox News |language=en-US |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Israeli president [[Isaac Herzog]] also paid tribute to Graham, calling him a "great American patriot" a "great friend of Israel", and a "beacon of moral clarity", and praising his support for the [[United States–Israel relations|U.S.–Israel partnership]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Berman |first=Lazar |date=July 12, 2026 |title= Herzog, Katz mourn US Sen. Lindsey Graham: 'A beacon of moral clarity' |url= https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/herzog-katz-mourn-us-sen-lindsey-graham-a-beacon-of-moral-clarity/ |website=The Times of Israel |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Defense Minister [[Israel Katz]] said Graham "stood with Israel in its most difficult moments" and was an unwavering supporter of Israel's security and its alliance with the United States.<ref>{{cite web |title= Beloved by all: Israeli leaders mourn true friend Sen. Lindsey Graham |url= https://www.israelnationalnews.com/news/430010 |website= Israel National News |date=July 12, 2026 |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref> Former Israeli ambassador [[Michael Oren]] said Graham's death was a "diplomatic and strategic loss" for Israel, while Iranian state media called Graham a "warmongering and anti-Iranian" senator.<ref name="AP-2026" /> Iranian opposition leader [[Reza Pahlavi]] said: "His support for Iran's Lion and Sun Revolution earned him the title 'Uncle Lindsey' among Iranians. He will be remembered with profound gratitude and deep respect".<ref>{{cite web |title=Senator Graham was a steadfast friend of the Iranian people, Prince Pahlavi says |url=https://www.iranintl.com/en/202607127521 |website=Iran International |date=July 12, 2026 |access-date=July 12, 2026}}</ref>

Iranian state television aired a segment celebrating Graham's death and congratulating the Iranian people. The clip went viral on many Iranian outlets and social media accounts, which long viewed him as a leading advocate of military action against Tehran. During the [[state funeral of Ali Khamenei]] a week earlier, demonstrators carried signs with Graham's image alongside other figures as future assassination targets. Iranian social media accounts posted images of his face crossed out with a red X on a graphic representing the perpetrators of the [[2026 Iran war|United States attacks on Iran]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ben Ari |first=Lior |date=July 12, 2026 |title='So sweet I'll read it twice': Iran celebrates Lindsey Graham's death |url=https://www.ynetnews.com/article/skuhxxbegg |work=Ynetglobal |language=en |access-date=July 13, 2026}}</ref> [[Spokesperson for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Iran|Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman]] [[Esmail Baghaei]] said of Graham's death, "the Angel of Death is just" and "for such a person, whose entire record is one of malice and support for aggression, nothing but a black record will remain in the minds of people. [...] the death of this radical senator will certainly not sadden the heart of any free person."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Konig |first=Joseph |date=July 13, 2026 |title=Iran Says 'the Angel of Death Is Just' in Scathing Reaction to 'Hot-Headed' Sen. Lindsey Graham's Fatal Medical Emergency |url=https://people.com/iran-scathing-response-lindsey-graham-death-12017191 |website=People |language=en-US |access-date= July 14, 2026}}</ref>

=== Memorial services ===
President Trump ordered flags lowered in observance of Graham's death.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Death of Senator Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/07/death-of-senator-lindsey-graham/ |website=The White House |language=en-US |date=July 13, 2026 |access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref> Governor Henry McMaster also issued an executive order to have flags lowered in South Carolina.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rigby |first=Tiffany |date=July 15, 2026 |title=SC governor orders flags atop state capitol be lowered to half-staff in honor of Sen. Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.abccolumbia.com/2026/07/15/sc-governor-orders-flags-atop-state-capitol-be-lowered-to-half-staff-in-honor-of-sen-lindsey-graham/ |website=ABC Columbia |language=en-US |access-date=July 22, 2026}}</ref>

==== Washington, D.C. service ====
A memorial service for Graham took place on July 28 in [[Washington, D.C.]].<ref name= "Roll Call-2026">{{cite web |title= Dates set for Lindsey Graham funeral services |url= https://rollcall.com/2026/07/17/dates-set-lindsey-graham-funeral/ |website= Roll Call |date= July 17, 2026 |access-date= July 17, 2026}}</ref>

At an arrival ceremony at the [[U.S. Capitol]], Graham was honored in the Capitol Rotunda. U.S. House Chaplain [[Margaret Kibben]] opened the Rotunda. Representative [[Sheri Biggs]], Senate Majority Leader [[John Thune]], and Vice President [[JD Vance]] gave eulogies, and, after a military hymn, U.S. Senate chaplain [[Barry C. Black]] led closing prayer. Darline Graham and Senate Minority Leader [[Chuck Schumer]] were among those in attendance.<ref>{{cite news |title= Lindsey Graham funeral updates: SC senator remembered in tearful ceremony |url= https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/07/28/lindsey-graham-funeral-live-updates--live/91062645007/ |work= USA Today |language= en-US |date= July 28, 2026 |access-date= July 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Carpenter |first= Susan |date= July 28, 2026 |title= Vance and Thune pay tribute to the late GOP Sen. Lindsey Graham in Washington |url= https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/rochester/politics/2026/07/28/sen--lindsey-graham-capitol-rotunda-service |work= Spectrum News |language= en-US |access-date= July 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Rimmer |first= Morgan |date= July 28, 2026 |title= Vance and Thune pay tribute to the late GOP Sen. Lindsey Graham in Washington - Where are attendees sitting at the Capitol ceremony for Lindsey Graham? |url= https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/28/politics/live-news/lindsey-graham-funeral |work= CNN |language= en-US |access-date= July 28, 2026}}</ref>[[File:President Donald J. Trump attends the funeral service for South Carolina Senator Lindsey Graham (55428016767).jpg|thumb|President [[Donald Trump]], Vice President [[JD Vance]], Treasury Secretary [[Scott Bessent]], businessman [[Eric Trump]], and Justice [[Brett Kavanaugh]] attending Graham's funeral service on July 28, 2026]]
Later that afternoon, Graham's casket arrived in the [[Washington National Cathedral]], where his funeral was held. [[Randy Hollerith]], the dean of the Washington National Cathedral, presided over the service, with passages from Scripture also read by [[Tony Perkins (politician)|Tony Perkins]], [[Marjorie Dannenfelser]], and [[Franklin Graham]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Paulsen |first=David |date=July 28, 2026 |title=Trump honors Lindsey Graham as 'a giant of the US Senate' at National Cathedral funeral |url=https://episcopalnewsservice.org/2026/07/28/trump-honors-lindsey-graham-as-a-giant-of-the-us-senate-at-national-cathedral-funeral/ |work= Episcopal News Service |language=en-US | access-date= 28 July 2026}}</ref> President Trump delivered a eulogy, as did Fox News presenter [[Sean Hannity]], Graham's niece Emilie Boggs Roberts, and Tim Tate, the lead pastor of Corinth Baptist Church in [[Seneca, South Carolina]], who was Graham's personal pastor.<ref>{{cite news |title= Live Updates: Lindsey Graham Remembered at Funeral as Friend and Relentless Fighter |url= https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/07/28/us/lindsey-graham-funeral-services#with-lindsey-graham-gone-ukraine-loses-an-ally-with-trumps-ear |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |date=July 28, 2026 |access-date=July 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Rimmer |first=Morgan |date=July 28, 2026 |title=Vance and Thune pay tribute to the late GOP Sen. Lindsey Graham in Washington - Graham's pastor praises his "moral compass," adding that it sometimes made the senator unpopular |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/28/politics/live-news/lindsey-graham-funeral |work=CNN |language=en-US |access-date=July 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Watson |first1=Kathryn |last2=Quinn |first2=Melissa |last3=Watson |first3=Eleanor |last4=Hubbard |first4=Kaia |last5= Yilek |first5=Caitlin |date=July 28, 2026 |title= Trump calls Sen. Lindsey Graham an "American original" at funeral as lawmakers, world leaders pay respects |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/lindsey-graham-funeral-trump-washington-dc/ |work=CBS News |language=en-US |access-date=July 28, 2026}}</ref> Israeli Prime Minister [[Benjamin Netanyahu]], Ukrainian President [[Volodymyr Zelenskyy]], and Finnish President [[Alexander Stubb]] attended the afternoon service.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hubbard |first1=Kaia |date=July 28, 2026 |title=Watch Live: Sen. Lindsey Graham remembered in Washington ceremonies today |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/lindsey-graham-funeral-trump-eulogy/ |work=CBS News |language=en-US |access-date=July 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Donald Trump and world leaders attend Lindsey Graham's funeral |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/gallery/2026/7/28/donald-trump-and-world-leaders-attend-lindsey-grahams-funeral |work=Al Jazeera |date=July 28, 2026 |access-date=July 28, 2026}}</ref>

==== Columbia service ====
On July 29, Graham was honored at the [[South Carolina State House]] in Columbia, South Carolina, with a flyover as his funeral procession made its way to First Baptist Church. Graham's funeral was open to the public, but his burial service in [[Pickens County, South Carolina]], was private.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Wong |first1= Scott |last2= Thorp V |first2= Frank |date= July 28, 2026 |title= Washington says goodbye to Sen. Lindsey Graham, a 'true American original' |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/washington-says-goodbye-sen-lindsey-graham-rcna589475 |work= NBC News |language= en-US |access-date= 28 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Cervantes |first= Fernando Jr. |date= July 27, 2026 |title= When is Lindsay Graham's funeral services? See dates, plans |url= https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/07/27/lindsey-graham-funeral-services-dates-plans/91067000007/ |work= USA Today |language= en-US |access-date= July 27, 2026}}</ref>

Attendees included Attorney General of South Carolina [[Alan Wilson (politician)|Alan Wilson]], State Representative [[Jermaine Johnson (politician)|Jermaine Johnson]], former Ambassador to Canada [[David H. Wilkins]], former Congressman [[Henry Brown (South Carolina politician)|Henry Brown]],<ref>{{Cite web |last= Press |first= The Associated |date= 2026-07-29 |title= Lindsey Graham returns home one last time for his funeral in South Carolina |url= https://www.wistv.com/2026/07/29/live-sen-lindsey-graham-honored-with-memorial-service-south-carolina/ |website= WIS-TV |language= en-US |access-date= July 29, 2026}}</ref> Lieutenant Governor [[Pamela Evette]], U.S. Representatives [[Ralph Norman]] and [[Sheri Biggs]], former South Carolina Governors [[Nikki Haley]] and [[Mark Sanford]], Fox News host [[Sean Hannity]],<ref>{{Cite web |last1= Franco |first1= Jose |last2= Maultsby |first2=Baker |last3= Cronin |first3=Ruth |last4=Carpentier |first4=Bella |date=July 29, 2026 |title=Gov. McMaster, South Carolinians honor Lindsey Graham at SC service {{!}} Live |url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/politics/2026/07/29/lindsey-graham-services-in-south-carolina-today-live/91075952007/ |website=The Greenville News |language=en-US |access-date= July 29, 2026}}</ref> Treasury Secretary [[Scott Bessent]], Judge [[J. Michelle Childs]], South Carolina House Speaker [[Murrell Smith Jr.]], State Senator [[Karl B. Allen]], and preservationist [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]].<ref>{{Cite web |date= July 29, 2026 |title=Funeral service of Lindsey Graham |url=https://x.com/Bruce4USSenate/status/2082573546820174024 |access-date=July 29, 2026 |website=Twitter/X}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham's S.C. funeral attended by state dignitaries, family |url=https://www.upi.com/Top_News/US/2026/07/29/sen-lindsey-graham-south-carolina-funeral/7711785354475/ |website=UPI |language=en-US |access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref>

Speakers included Governor [[Henry McMaster]], Congressman [[Jim Clyburn]], Senator [[Tim Scott]], ''[[Fox & Friends]]'' co-host [[Ainsley Earhardt]], and former congressman [[Trey Gowdy]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Reynolds |first1=Nicholas |last2=Grenier |first2=Ian |last3=Bozard |first3=Caleb |last4=Connor |first4=Eric |last5=Kropf |first5=Schuyler |last6=Lawrence |first6=Jordan |date=July 29, 2026 |title=Live updates: Graham funeral comes to a close in Columbia. Burial to follow in Pickens. |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/lindsey-grahahm-funeral-columbia-pickens-sc/article_cb102a4d-d206-4c8f-b520-3e2cff928750.html |website=Post and Courier |language=en |access-date=July 29, 2026}}</ref>

Graham was buried in Mt. Zion Cemetery in [[Central, South Carolina]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=King |first=Kimberly |date=2026-07-30 |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham laid to rest in hometown, community pays respects |url=https://wlos.com/news/local/lindsey-graham-funeral-hometown-senator-south-carolina-residents-procession-community-pickens-county-reaction-politics-government-central-pendleton-darline-family-neighbors-career |access-date=2026-07-30 |website=WLOS |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Erbach |first1=Alexa |title=Residents visit Central, S.C. cemetery to pay respects to Lindsey Graham ahead of Wednesday burial |url=https://www.foxcarolina.com/2026/07/28/residents-visit-central-sc-cemetery-pay-respects-lindsey-graham-ahead-wednesday-burial/ |access-date=29 July 2026 |work=foxcarolina.com |agency=Fox Carolina |date=28 July 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Legacy and memorials ===
On August 10, 2026, [[Joint Base Charleston]] was renamed Joint Base Lindsey Graham. Present at the naming ceremony were US Senator [[Darline Graham]], South Carolina Governor [[Henry McMaster]], Treasury Secretary [[Scott Bessent]] and Secretary of Defense [[Pete Hegseth]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gordon |first=Chris |date=2026-08-11 |title=Key C-17 Base in South Carolina Renamed After Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.airandspaceforces.com/key-c-17-base-south-carolina-renamed-joint-base-lindsey-graham/ |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=Air & Space Forces Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=August 10, 2026 |title=WATCH LIVE: Joint Base Charleston renamed for Sen. Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/watch-live-hegseth-speaks-at-joint-base-lindsey-graham-renaming-ceremony |access-date=2026-08-11 |website=PBS}}</ref>

On August 23, 2026, the [[University of South Carolina]] announced its intention to name its planned new ROTC building the Senator Lindsey Graham ROTC Center of Excellence.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Koeske |first=Zak |date=August 23, 2026 |title=USC to name new building for late Lindsey Graham, a two-time Carolina alum |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/local/article316940318.html#campaignName=columbia_morning_newsletter |access-date=August 24, 2026 |work=The State Newspaper}}</ref>

On September 19, 2026, US President Donald Trump signed the "[[Sanctioning Russia Act|Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026]]," which allows him to impose tariffs of up to 100 percent on Russian oil buyers, including China and India, in response to the [[Russo-Ukrainian war (2022–present)|Russian invasion of Ukraine]], as well as to extend [[United States sanctions against Iran|existing sanctions on Iran]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Trump signs sweeping Russia sanctions over Ukraine war |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/19/trump-signs-sweeping-russia-sanctions-over-ukraine-war |website= Al Jazeera |date=19 September 2026 |access-date=20 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Trump signs Russia-Iran sanctions law with 100% tariff threat for India, China |url=https://www.indiatoday.in/world/story/trump-signs-russia-iran-sanctions-law-100-percent-tariffs-russian-oil-buyers-ptag-2998063-2026-09-19 |website= India Today |date=19 September 2026 |access-date=20 September 2026}}</ref>

